BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:What is Artificial Intelligence?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T193000
UID:6ac6476ae06996003a81e2f9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:AI technologies have become increasingly prevalent in many asp
 ects of modern life\, including healthcare\, finance\, transportation\, an
 d entertainment. Some examples of AI applications include virtual assistan
 ts like Siri and Alexa\, self-driving cars\, fraud detection systems\, and
  personalized recommendations in online shopping. Come join this beginner 
 in-person class to learn what AI is\, how it learns and predicts outcomes\
 , considerations when using GenAI applications\, and how the Internet of T
 hings (IOT) applies to AI.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal i
 nformation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under t
 he Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informat
 ion will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and ev
 aluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be 
 directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 
 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Forest Hill\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac6476ae06996003a81e2f9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artists in the Library: Origami Lotus Flowers
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T180000
UID:6aac152519094f0c6938b75a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This is a six-part workshop series that creates a space for ex
 ploring belonging and not belonging through conversation and hands-on art-
 making. Guided by reflective questions\, participants engage in practices 
 such as origami with washi\, drawing\, dyeing\, and writing\, using the lo
 tus as a shared symbol of transformation and resilience across cultures. T
 he workshops foster an inclusive\, supportive environment\, particularly f
 or those who feel marginalized or disconnected. The series culminates in a
  collaborative public floral installation in the library\, sharing partici
 pants’ work with the wider community.\nLed by artists "Being~inbetween"\
 nThe workshops are open to youth\, ages 13-17\, and require no prior artis
 tic experience.\nWorkshop Dates:\nFriday\, September 11th\nFriday\, Septem
 ber 18th\nFriday\, October 30th\nFriday\, November 6th\nFriday\, December 
 4th\nFriday\, December 11th
LOCATION:Thorncliffe\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aac152519094f0c6938b75a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
