BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Information Battle: Elections\, Journalism and Misinformation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T193000
UID:6a9dc0591c197d11325dc656@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:As elections take place in an increasingly complicated online 
 world\, voters are exposed to a huge mix of news\, social media posts\, AI
 -generated content\, and false information spread by organized groups. New
  digital technologies have made it easier for people to learn about issues
  and take part in civic life\, but they have also raised concerns about tr
 ust\, fair elections\, and respectful public discussion.\nThis panel bring
 s together experts in misinformation\, journalism\, and election administr
 ation to explore the challenges and opportunities facing democracy today. 
 How are AI and new technologies changing the way people get information? W
 hat do these changes mean for voters\, journalists\, and election official
 s? And what can be done to build public trust in elections while helping p
 eople stay informed and engaged in public life?\nFeaturing\nNick Hune-Brow
 n\nJournalist and Author\nAward-winning magazine writer and Executive Edit
 or at The Local\nValérie Kindarji\nPolitical scientist\nUniversity of Tor
 onto focused on the impacts of disinformation on democracies in Europe and
  North America and Lab Manager at the University of British Columbia.\nTob
 ias Novogrodsky\nDeputy City Clerk\nElection Services\, City of Toronto\nM
 oderated by:\nAnita Li\nCEO and editor-in-chief of The Green Line\nToronto
 's municipal election takes place on October 26\, 2026. Check out the\nKno
 wYourVote.TO\nwebsite and the\nCelebrate Democracy\npage to learn more abo
 ut the upcoming election\, access important voter resources\, and discover
  activities and interactive library programs for all ages happening at a b
 ranch near you.\nThis initiative is made possible by donations to the Toro
 nto Public Library Foundation and is generously supported by the McConnell
  Foundation.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on 
 this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libr
 aries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only 
 be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Ques
 tions about how the library handles your information can be directed to An
 swerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\,
  Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9dc0591c197d11325dc656
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Introduction to Fabric Cross Stitch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T120000
UID:6aba9e71295b3b0033c9e069@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Come stitch back and relax with Yen at YH Cross Stitch Designs
 .  We will learn how to do counted cross stitch on fabric with a beginner-
 friendly original design from her collection that includes nature\, floral
 \, book\, and food & beverage designs.  Participants will receive a wooden
  embroidery hoop\, AIDA fabric\, needle\, thread\, instruction booklet\, a
 nd pattern design to start their project and complete at home.\nInformatio
 n Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected b
 y the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(
 a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pr
 ogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswe
 rline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:New Toronto
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aba9e71295b3b0033c9e069
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
